<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jingzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jingzhou is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Near it lie the ruins of Ji'nancheng, usually identified as Ying, the capital of the State of Chu, and the area is rich in Chu tombs. In later centuries Jingzhou was a strategic fortress of the middle Yangtze, fought over in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>, and its Ming-era city walls survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jingzhou.jpg" alt="Jingzhou" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Popolon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muraille_Est_de_Jingzhou.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
